{# What the site tells the apps, and the one way it tells them.

   The apps -- oeee-cafe-apple (iOS and macOS), oeee-cafe-android, and
   oeee-cafe-desktop (Windows, on Steam and the Microsoft Store) -- are web
   views around this site. Each used to learn what it needed by reading the
   page: a CSS class on the unread badge, the painter's paths, which images
   were drawings, a pixel under the status bar. Every one of those broke the
   day the site changed and nothing said so; the unread badge on Windows and
   macOS read 0 for weeks after the bell stopped rendering `.toolbar-badge`.
   So the site says these things itself, and the apps listen.

   The channel is one object called `oeeeBridge`, which each app registers
   before the page's first script runs:

     iOS, macOS   webkit.messageHandlers.oeeeBridge (WKScriptMessageHandler)
     Android      window.oeeeBridge (WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener,
                  restricted to the site's origin)
     Windows      window.oeeeBridge, an init script whose postMessage emits
                  the Tauri event `oeee-bridge` with the string as payload

   Every message is a JSON string -- a string because that is all Android's
   listener carries -- of an object with `v` (this contract's version, 1) and
   `type`. The apps ignore types they do not know and fields they do not
   know, so the site can add either without a release of any of them.

   page     {path, signedIn, presence, community, group, join, seats, party,
             since, painting, refreshable}
            On every page, and again whenever any of it changes: a boosted
            navigation, a back swipe that restored the page, someone coming
            into or leaving a collaborative room. `signedIn` is
            null on a page without the toolbar, which cannot tell. `presence`
            is presence_meta.jinja's activity, or null for browsing; `join`
            is the path a friend asked into a collaborative room opens, with
            its `seats` and the `party` in it now, all three null on any
            other page and in a room a friend could not enter; `since` is
            when the room opened, in milliseconds since the epoch, for the
            clock beside a Discord activity, null outside a room;
            `painting` is whether leaving would lose a drawing in progress,
            and `refreshable` whether pulling down may reload the page --
            neither a painter nor a replay may be.
   unread   {count}
            The number on the bell (nav_notifications.jinja): unread
            notifications and invitations waiting, together. Sent whenever it
            changes, which is when a request swaps the bell.
   theme    {choice, ground, grid, toolbar}
            `choice` is "light", "dark" or "system", as the reader picked it
            in the toolbar; what "system" comes to is the app's to know.
            `ground` and `grid` are the design system's --ds-ground and
            --ds-grid (ds.css), as CSS colours: the field behind every page,
            the toolbar and the painter's included, and the lines ruled on
            it every 14px. An app draws the same field wherever it draws
            against the page -- its bars, under an overscroll, behind the
            window before the page arrives -- rather than keep its own copy
            of NEO's colours in step with this one. `toolbar` is the
            toolbar's own ground (--ds-toolbar), which is the page's today
            but need not be: what an app draws directly above the toolbar --
            the strip behind the status bar -- is this, so the two read as
            one bar. Each is null on a page without the design system's
            stylesheet.
   words    {leaveTitle, leaveBody, leave, stay, saveImage, copyImage,
             share, copyLink, savedImage, savedFile, saveFailed,
             presenceBrowsing, presenceDrawing, presenceRelaying,
             presenceCollaborating, presenceDrawingBanner,
             presenceWatchingReplay}
            What an app says in dialogs and menus of its own over the page
            -- the leave prompt, the long press on a drawing, the right-click
            menu -- and on Discord, what the reader is doing for each
            presence_meta.jinja activity, in the page's language, from the
            site's catalogues (locales/*.ftl, the app-* messages). Sent once
            a page, since the reader can change language between pages. An
            app keeps words of its own only for what it says with no page to
            speak for it: a site that cannot be reached.
   haptic   {name}
            "light", "medium", "selection", "success", "warning" or "error":
            a control that is felt as well as seen.
   pressed  {drawing: {src, link, width, height} | null, sideways}
            As a finger lands, whether it is on a drawing -- an image marked
            data-oeee-drawing, which is never a sensitive one -- so an app
            that opens its own menu on a long press knows which in time.
            `link` is the post's page, or "" on the post page itself.
            `sideways` is whether a drag to the right from there is the
            page's own: a slider, a drawing zoomed closer (touch-action
            none), or a row scrolled along that can scroll back. An app that
            goes back on a swipe from anywhere leaves those to the page.
   painter  {state: "ready"}
            The painter can be driven: oeeeApp.painter.command(name) and
            .preferPen() (frontend/shared/appBridge.ts).
   prices   {products}
   purchase {product}
   restore  {}
            The Supporter Pack, on /supporter, through the store the build
            sells through (data-store, below): what the products cost, sell
            one, hand over what was already bought. The products are the
            site's catalogue, and the server draws only the build's own
            store's buttons, so every one asked about is the app's to sell.
            The app answers with oeeeApp.store.prices and
            oeeeApp.store.purchased, and every store goes the same way
            (app_store.jinja).
   signIn   {provider, nonce?}
   browse   {url}
            A sign-in the page is carrying for an app: make an ID token for
            `nonce` with the platform's own sheet (Apple, Google), a ticket
            from Steam (provider "steam", no nonce), or open `url` in the
            system's browser: this site's, or Google's or Discord's sign-in
            page, which an app checks it is (app_sign_in.jinja).
   password {id}
            The Mac app: the reader went to the username or password of a
            form that takes a saved one. The app offers the system's saved
            passwords for the site in a sheet of its own
            (app_saved_password.jinja).
   notify   {title, body, url}
            Windows, which is sent no push: a new notification for the
            reader, heard live (live.jinja) -- in its reader's language, `url`
            a path on this site. The app shows it as the system's
            notification and, pressed, opens `url` in the window. The page
            shows it as a toast as well, as it does in every app.
   discord  {action, user?}
            Windows, with Discord: the reader's Discord account in the app
            (discord.rs in oeee-cafe-desktop). "connect" it -- Discord's
            consent screen, once -- or "disconnect" it; "friends" for the
            room's invite picker, answered now and as they change; "invite"
            `user`, a Discord id as a string (a number cannot hold one), to
            the room showing; "letIn" a `user` who asked to join it -- their
            request answered with an invitation -- or "dismiss" their
            request, which Discord is not told of. Asked only on a page the app has told
            discord.state (below), so never where nothing would answer.
   steam    {action, join}
            The Steam build of the Windows app (data-store="steam"):
            "invite" opens Steam's own invitation over the window, the
            reader's Steam friends to ask into the room `join` opens -- the
            page's `join`, above. A friend who accepts has Steam open their
            own app on it (steam.rs in oeee-cafe-desktop). Nothing answers.
   share    {title, text}
   download {name, mime, data}
            What Android's web view cannot do itself (app_polyfills.jinja).

   Each app ends its web view's user agent with  OeeeCafe platform/<app>,
   and a build that sells adds  store/<store>, before or after the platform
   but in the same run of words. theme_head.jinja reads that in
   one place and marks the root before the page paints: `data-app` (which
   app: "ios", "android", "macos", "windows"), `data-form` (the kind of
   device: "handheld" or "desktop") and `data-store` (where it sells). The
   site owns what those marks change, the room kept for a window's own
   controls included (ds.css); an app says what it is and nothing about the
   site's layout, and injects nothing to say it.

   window.oeeeApp.wouldLoseWork() is whether leaving now would lose
   something: the page's own `beforeunload` handlers, asked the way a
   browser asks them, which neither WKWebView nor a window being closed
   asks by itself. The painter answers only for a drawing with something in
   it, and not while it is being saved -- which `painting` above cannot say.
   When the app has asked the reader itself -- after wouldLoseWork(), or
   from its web view's own `beforeunload` dialog -- and the answer was
   Leave, it calls window.oeeeApp.leaving() and begins the load once the
   promise settles; the page shows it is on its way, as it does for every
   load it sees coming. Every app asks both the same way, in the words
   appContract.json gives them.

   A build that sells says which store it sells through in its user agent
   -- store/apple (the App Store), store/google (Google Play),
   store/microsoft (the Microsoft Store) or store/steam -- so `data-store`
   is on the root before the page paints, early enough that Steam's
   sign-in is never drawn in a build that would do nothing with it. The server reads the same words to choose
   /supporter's buttons (Store::from_user_agent in models/supporter.rs,
   the same rule), so those are never in the page at all. It is the build
   that says so, not the platform: the Microsoft Store build of the Windows
   app is not the Steam one. A build that says nothing is offered nothing
   to press.

   The other way, the apps call the page, and everything they call is on
   one object, window.oeeeApp, which this template makes. The parts of the
   page that answer an app add themselves to it as they load, and a page
   without them simply lacks the member, so an app asks for one it may not
   find as `window.oeeeApp && oeeeApp.command && oeeeApp.command(name)`:

     wouldLoseWork()         here; see below
     leaving()               here; see below
     pushToken(token)        here: the app's push token, from APNs or FCM,
                             which the app hands over whenever a page says
                             someone is signed in. The page registers it for
                             them (POST /devices, from its own session,
                             with the platform its user agent names), once a
                             document; the answer sets the oeee_device cookie
                             the site's sign-out reads (devices.rs, auth.rs).
                             The app makes no request to the site itself.
     signIn.answer(told), signIn.resume(), signIn.unopened()
                             app_sign_in.jinja
     password.answer(told)   app_saved_password.jinja: the password picked,
                             with the id it was asked with
     command(name)           toolbar.jinja: the site's menu commands and keys,
                             true when the page knew the name
     store.prices(prices)    supporter.jinja
     store.purchased(proofs) app_store.jinja: proof of what the store sold,
                             answered with the proofs the site took -- for
                             the Microsoft Store, a Store ID key, which the
                             app gets from GetCustomerCollectionsIdAsync
                             (ticket, user)
     store.ticket()          app_store.jinja: {ticket, user} for that call,
                             or null outside the Microsoft Store build
     painter.command(name), painter.preferPen()
                             frontend/shared/appBridge.ts, on a drawing page
     discord.state(linked), discord.friends(list), discord.requests(list),
     discord.invited(result)
                             here: the reader's Discord account in the
                             Windows app, which tells every page it shows
                             {connected, connecting, name}; the friends it
                             was asked for, [{id, name, status, playing}];
                             in a room, the friends who pressed Ask to Join
                             in Discord and are waiting, [{id, name}], again
                             whenever that changes; and {user, sent} for an
                             invitation, letting someone in included. Kept for
                             what loads after (discord.known()) and said as
                             the oeee:discord event, for the room
                             (frontend/shared/appBridge.ts) and the account
                             page (account_discord_app.jinja).

   And --oeee-text-scale on the root, which is CSS rather than a call: the
   Android app sets it; on iOS the page measures it itself (theme_head.jinja).

   In the desktop apps, whose toolbar is the window's title bar, the page
   also sends
     window    {action}  the Mac's "drag" or "zoom": the toolbar's empty
               space pressed, or double-clicked, as a title bar is
               (theme_head.jinja); Windows's "minimize", "maximize" (to
               restore, too) and "close", its caption buttons
               (app_caption.jinja)
     caption   {place}   Windows: where the maximise button is, in the
               window's pixels ({x, y, width, height}, or null), for the
               app's Snap Layouts stand-in
   and the Windows app answers with
     caption({maximized, pointer})
                             app_caption.jinja: whether the window is
                             maximised, and "hot" or "pressed" while the
                             pointer is on the stand-in, "" when not

   There is no other channel. The web-view apps were never released before
   this contract; the builds people have from before it are the native
   apps, which never load these pages.

   The apps test against frontend/shared/appContract.json: the user agents
   and the marks each makes, an example of every message above as the page
   sends it, every member of oeeeApp, the names command() takes, and the
   two calls around leaving, word for word. appContract.browser.test.ts
   captures the page again and fails when the file no longer says what it
   does, so a change here that an app has to follow shows up as a change to
   that file, which each app fetches with its scripts/sync-app-contract.sh.

   The script is frontend/site/bridge.ts, in chrome.js (theme_head.jinja);
   the words are the one thing it is handed from here. #}
<script>
  if (window.oeeeChrome) window.oeeeChrome.bridge({
    leaveTitle: {{ ftl_get_message("app-leave-title")|tojson }},
    leaveBody: {{ ftl_get_message("app-leave-body")|tojson }},
    leave: {{ ftl_get_message("app-leave")|tojson }},
    stay: {{ ftl_get_message("app-stay")|tojson }},
    saveImage: {{ ftl_get_message("app-save-image")|tojson }},
    copyImage: {{ ftl_get_message("app-copy-image")|tojson }},
    share: {{ ftl_get_message("app-share")|tojson }},
    copyLink: {{ ftl_get_message("app-copy-link")|tojson }},
    savedImage: {{ ftl_get_message("app-saved-image")|tojson }},
    savedFile: {{ ftl_get_message("app-saved-file")|tojson }},
    saveFailed: {{ ftl_get_message("app-save-failed")|tojson }},
    presenceBrowsing: {{ ftl_get_message("app-presence-browsing")|tojson }},
    presenceDrawing: {{ ftl_get_message("app-presence-drawing")|tojson }},
    presenceRelaying: {{ ftl_get_message("app-presence-relaying")|tojson }},
    presenceCollaborating: {{ ftl_get_message("app-presence-collaborating")|tojson }},
    presenceDrawingBanner: {{ ftl_get_message("app-presence-drawing-banner")|tojson }},
    presenceWatchingReplay: {{ ftl_get_message("app-presence-watching-replay")|tojson }}
  });
</script>
